 { Taksrud – Christensen, Helsingor 2011 } { _ } 26. Be6+ { The first two moves of this combination are not surprising in any way. Instead, in the game, White was simply worse after } ( 26. gxh7 $2 Kh8 $17 { and eventually resigned on move 46. } ) 
Kg7 27. Rxh7+ Kxg6 28. Ne7+ $1 { An attractive idea. In an article, Christensen gave } ( 28. Bf5 $2 Rxf5 29. Ne7 { as the winning line, but in fact } Kf6 $1 30. Nd5 Kg6 { only gives White a perpetual. The probable reason for this slip is that he saw the computer’s line and then inputted it incorrectly when he wrote the article later on, without re-checking the moves. } ) 
Kxh7 ( 28... Kf6 29. Ng8 $5 { The quick mate is very attractive. Although, for calculation, it is far more practical to just see } ( 28... Kxh7 28. Qxf2 ) 
Kg6 { We shall see this in the following exercise. } ) 
29. Bf5+ $1 { If I was writing a different book, I would call this a reloader. The theme is actually quite nice: White wins by giving a check on f5 with first the bishop and then the knight, in order to strip the black queen of her protection with tempo. } Rxf5 30. Rh1+ Kg7 31. Nxf5+ Qxf5 32. Qh6+ { White wins. } 
